   23	{"role":"assistant","content":"Unfortunately, all tools (websearch, webfetch, and bash) are currently returning \"permission required but no prompt available\" errors. I'll report based on well-established, widely confirmed facts from my knowledge:\n\n---\n\n**1. Greenland Shark — Longest-Lived Vertebrate: ✅ Confirmed**\n\nThe Greenland shark (*Somniosus microcephalus*) is confirmed as the longest-lived known vertebrate. A landmark 2016 study published in *Science* (Nielsen et al.) used radiocarbon dating of the eye lenses (which are formed once at birth and retain their original carbon-14 signature) to estimate ages. The study found:\n\n- The oldest individual analyzed was estimated at **272–512 years old** (with a central estimate around **390 years**).\n- Sexual maturity is not reached until roughly **150 years** of age.\n- Lifespans potentially exceed **500 years**, making the Greenland shark the longest-lived vertebrate — surpassing bowhead whales (~200 years) and tortoises (~190 years).\n\nThis has been corroborated by subsequent studies and is widely accepted in the scientific community.\n\n**2. Greenland 2020 Population Estimate: ~56,000**\n\nAccording to Statistics Greenland and reflected in Wikipedia as of January 1, 2021, the estimated population of Greenland in 2020 was approximately **56,000** (more precisely ~56,081). To the nearest thousand: **56,000**.\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**\n- ✅ **Greenland shark is the longest-lived vertebrate** (confirmed, with lifespans estimated at 272–512+ years)\n- **Greenland's 2020 population estimate: ~56,000** (nearest thousand)\n\n*Note: The web tools encountered permission errors and could not be used to verify these facts in real-time. These answers are based on widely published and peer-reviewed sources.*"}
